1. c4 e6 2. Nf3 d5 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 Be7 5. O-O O-O 6. b3 c5 7. Bb2 Nc6 8. e3 b6 9. Qe2 Ba6 10. d3 Qd7 (10... Qc7 {is perhaps the more natural choice of square.}) 11. e4 Rad8 12. Nc3 ({Allowing Black a spatial advantage in the centre.} 12. e5 Ne8 13. Nc3 Nc7 14. Rfd1 b5 15. a4 b4 16. Nb5 {was equal in Wolf-Brueggemann, Bundesliga 1993.}) 12... d4 13. Nb1 e5 14. Nh4 g6 15. f4 exf4 16. gxf4 Ng4 $5 17. Bh3 Bxh4 18. Bxg4 f5 19. Bh3 Qc7 {?} 20. exf5 ({This plan proves to be too risky. White is safe enough after} 20. e5) 20... gxf5 21. Qh5 Qe7 22. Bxf5 $2 {?} Rxf5 $1 {[It seems incredible that Timman's sense of danger did not alert him to this shot, young as he was. The open g-file and a8-h1 diagonals, together with the disappearance of the light-squared white bishop, all add up to huge trouble on g2.]} 23. Qxf5 Qg7+ 24. Kh1 Bb7 25. Qe4 ({There is no viable defence.} 25. Rg1 {allows} Ne5+ 26. Qe4 Bxe4+ 27. dxe4 {and} Ng6 {saves Black's queen.}) ({After} 25. Rf3 {Black coolly plays} Kh8 $1 {followed by Rg8 and a mighty attack down the g-file.}) 25... Ne5 26. fxe5 Bxe4+ 27. dxe4 Qxe5 28. Nd2 Rf8 29. Rxf8+ Kxf8 30. Rf1+ Kg8 31. Rf5 {[Not the best choice, though White's chances of defending are slim anyway.]} Qe6 32. Bc1 Qh6 33. Rf1 Bg5 34. Rd1 Be3 35. b4 Qg5 36. Rf1 Qg4 37. Re1 h5 {Sources: Illustrated London News, 18 July 1970; CHESS, August 1970, p306; BCM, May 2005, p241. Scores: 1 VW Knox 5½/6, 2-7 LS Blackstock, LW Barden, H Bohm, ML Fuller, BR Eley, R Bellin 5; 8-15 J Timman, AJ Whiteley, B Cafferty, AT Ludgate, RA Beach, IJ Sinclair, T Bimpson, BH Hare 4½... players finishing down the field included N Littlewood, J Simpole, G Bonner, S Reuben, JT Farrand, PN Wallis, JJ Carleton, DG Ellison, TJ Beach (BCM, August 1970, p218)} 0-1

