{Rarely is a game so full of sustained excitement as this.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. e5 Nd5 4. Nc3 e6 5. Nxd5 exd5 6. d4 Nc6 ({More usual and reliable is} 6... d6) 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. Qxd5 Qb6 9. Bc4 Bxf2+ 10. Ke2 O-O 11. Rf1 Bc5 12. Ng5 Nxe5 13. Qxe5 ({Stockfish 17:} 13. Nxf7 $5 $16) 13... d5 14. Qxd5 Bg4+ 15. Rf3 Bg1 $2 ({Stockfish 17:} 15... Rad8 $11) 16. Nxf7 $18 Bxf3+ ({Rejecting} 16... Qf2+ {which could have led him into trouble after} 17. Kd3 {e.g.} Bxf3 18. Nh6+ Kh8 19. Qg8+ {and Black will be mated. It is extra ordinary how, for the next nine moves, Black manages to avoid any disaster from a discovered check.}) 17. Qxf3 ({Stockfish 17:} 17. gxf3 $18) 17... Rae8+ 18. Kd1 ({Stockfish 17:} 18. Kf1 $11) 18... Qd4+ 19. Qd3 Qg4+ 20. Kd2 Qf4+ ({Stockfish 17:} 20... Rxf7 $17) 21. Kc3 Qf6+ 22. Kb3 Qb6+ 23. Ka4 Qc6+ 24. Ka3 Rxf7 25. Bxf7+ ({Stockfish 17:} 25. Bf4 $14) 25... Kxf7 26. Bd2 Bxh2 {"It seems almost unbelievable that, after all these alarms, precise material equality should have been re-established. A draw would have been the logical outcome but Black went astray in time trouble and finally lost on time with two moves still to make." (BH Wood) Source: Illustrated London News, 1 August 1970} 1-0

